faqs:
  - question: "What is a one-click remote MCP server?"
    answer: "A remote MCP server is an HTTPS endpoint that AI tools such as Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, and VS Code call to work with a product on a user's behalf. One-click means the user pastes a URL or presses an Add button, signs in, approves access on a consent screen, and is connected. No client IDs, API keys, or config files change hands."
  - question: "What does a remote MCP server need for authentication?"
    answer: "The MCP authorization specification builds on OAuth 2.1. The server answers unauthenticated requests with a 401 that points to its protected resource metadata (RFC 9728), which names an authorization server. That server needs PKCE, discovery metadata, resource indicators so tokens are bound to one server, and a way for unknown clients to identify themselves through dynamic client registration or client ID metadata documents. The [Appwrite OAuth2 server](/docs/products/auth/oauth-server) implements all of these for your project."
  - question: "How does Claude connect to an MCP server without a pre-registered client ID?"
    answer: "Claude Code uses a client ID metadata document: its client ID is a URL it controls, and the authorization server fetches that URL to read the client's name and redirect URIs. Other clients use dynamic client registration and receive a client ID from a registration endpoint on the spot. An Appwrite project's discovery document advertises both."
  - question: "Is it safe to let any AI client register with my project?"
    answer: "Yes, because a client ID grants nothing on its own. It only lets a tool start an authorization request. The user still has to sign in to your product and approve the requested scopes on your consent screen, and the resulting access token is short-lived, bound to your MCP server's URL, and limited to those scopes."
  - question: "Can an MCP server run on Appwrite Functions?"
    answer: "Yes. An [Appwrite Function](/docs/products/functions) gets a public HTTPS domain on deploy, can serve the MCP endpoint and its metadata documents, and verifies access tokens offline against the project's JWKS. Functions return one response per request, so the server answers tool calls with JSON instead of a long-lived stream."
  - question: "How do I add a remote MCP server to my Appwrite project?"
    answer: "Connect your AI agent to the [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers) at `https://mcp.appwrite.io/`, sign in to your Appwrite account, and ask the agent to add a secure remote MCP server to your project. The agent enables the OAuth2 server, defines scopes, adds a consent page to your Site, and deploys the MCP endpoint as a Function. You do not configure anything yourself."
  - question: "Is the Appwrite Email app open source?"
    answer: "Yes. The full source, including the Site, the Functions, the MCP server, and the setup scripts, is at [github.com/appwrite/email](https://github.com/appwrite/email), and the live app runs at [email.appwrite.network](https://email.appwrite.network)."
